WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
This strawberry parent is connected to a smaller

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.560
plant by a long stem.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.720
No seed made the smaller plant.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.120
How can part of a stem become a

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.280
plant that can live separately?

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.680
We'll follow the connecting stem and then ask

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.000
why the new plant's own roots and leaves

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:16.640
matter.

00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:19.786
The long stem grows from the base of

00:00:19.827 --> 00:00:22.426
the parent and extends across the ground.

00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:24.626
We call this stem a runner.

00:00:24.907 --> 00:00:26.906
It is above the soil, while the roots

00:00:26.987 --> 00:00:28.346
grow down into it.

00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:30.906
The runner stays connected to the parent while

00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:32.266
the new plant develops.

00:00:32.747 --> 00:00:35.146
So we are following a stem route, not

00:00:35.227 --> 00:00:36.986
a root spreading sideways.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.960
A bud can develop at a point on

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.760
the runner called a node.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.080
In suitable growing conditions, it forms leaves above

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.200
the soil and roots below.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.640
New cells build these parts as the young

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:50.680
plant grows.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.640
Our illustration shows a successful later stage: the

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.000
small plant has its own roots and leaves

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:57.880
at that node.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.680
A tiny bud alone would not yet show

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.280
that it can live independently.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.420
Why do those new parts matter?

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.980
The roots take up water from the soil.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.700
The leaves use light to make food through

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:11.980
photosynthesis.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.100
Those resources support the young plant's growth.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.740
While it is developing, the connecting runner can

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:18.700
support it too.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.340
But once the plantlet is established with enough

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.820
roots and leaves, it can support itself.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.940
That is the reason it can become independent.

00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:30.526
Now imagine the connection is cut after the

00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:32.326
plantlet is established.

00:01:32.447 --> 00:01:34.326
Its roots are already in the soil, and

00:01:34.407 --> 00:01:36.246
it already has leaves.

00:01:36.287 --> 00:01:38.326
It can keep taking up water and making

00:01:38.407 --> 00:01:41.286
food, so it can continue growing separately when

00:01:41.527 --> 00:01:43.286
conditions remain suitable.

00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:46.086
Cutting the runner did not create the roots.

00:01:46.127 --> 00:01:48.726
The new plant needed those structures first.

00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:52.173
No sperm cell joined an egg cell to

00:01:52.294 --> 00:01:53.653
make this runner plant.

00:01:53.934 --> 00:01:55.613
The new plant grew from part of the

00:01:55.694 --> 00:01:59.053
parent, so this is asexual reproduction.

00:01:59.374 --> 00:02:02.853
You may also hear vegetative propagation: making a

00:02:02.894 --> 00:02:04.893
new plant from a parent part such as

00:02:04.974 --> 00:02:05.893
this stem.

00:02:06.254 --> 00:02:09.053
In the flower example, sperm and egg joining

00:02:09.134 --> 00:02:10.253
started the embryo.

00:02:10.734 --> 00:02:13.373
Here, roots and leaves developed on the runner

00:02:13.654 --> 00:02:14.173
instead.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.920
Try an earlier stage.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.640
A small bud on a strawberry runner has

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.360
begun growing leaves, but it has no roots

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:22.680
yet.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.000
It is still attached to the parent.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.800
Does that show it can already grow separately

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.080
if the connection is cut?

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.520
Explain using the resource it cannot yet take

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:33.560
up for itself.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.640
Pause and decide.

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:51.786
Not yet.

00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:54.666
The bud has leaves, but no roots to

00:02:54.747 --> 00:02:56.866
take up its own water from the soil.

00:02:57.147 --> 00:02:59.146
It still depends on its connection while it

00:02:59.227 --> 00:02:59.866
develops.

00:03:00.427 --> 00:03:03.226
It may grow roots later if conditions allow,

00:03:03.307 --> 00:03:05.706
but the supplied early stage does not show

00:03:05.747 --> 00:03:07.786
an established independent plant.

00:03:08.427 --> 00:03:11.226
That is why the order matters: roots and

00:03:11.307 --> 00:03:14.346
leaves develop before the plant can grow separately.

00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:19.166
Remember the reason, not just the word runner:

00:03:19.247 --> 00:03:22.046
roots and leaves first, separation later.

00:03:22.687 --> 00:03:24.926
The roots take up water, and the leaves

00:03:25.007 --> 00:03:26.446
make food in light.

00:03:26.767 --> 00:03:29.006
Once the new plant can support itself, it

00:03:29.087 --> 00:03:30.126
can grow separately.

00:03:30.767 --> 00:03:33.566
This stem route is asexual because sperm and

00:03:33.647 --> 00:03:34.926
egg did not join.

00:03:35.647 --> 00:03:37.886
Next, we will follow how bread mould can

00:03:38.047 --> 00:03:40.846
begin growing somewhere new using spores.

00:03:42.067 --> 00:03:44.706
If you would like notes, copy these now,

00:03:44.827 --> 00:03:46.226
or pause for longer.
